# Jittered points: geom_jitter

```elixir
Mix.install([
  {:giggly, path: Path.join(__DIR__, "..")},
  {:kino, "~> 0.19"}
  ])

alias Giggly.Plot

mpg = Giggly.Datasets.mpg()

render = fn plot ->
  plot
  |> Giggly.to_svg()
  |> IO.iodata_to_binary()
  |> Kino.Image.new(:svg)
end
```

## About this notebook

This notebook follows the examples on ggplot2's reference page for
[`geom_jitter()`](https://ggplot2.tidyverse.org/reference/geom_jitter.html), in the order the page
gives them. Each example shows ggplot2's code first and then Giggly's. An example Giggly cannot
draw yet keeps its ggplot2 code and names what it is waiting for, so the page is also a list of
what remains.

`geom_jitter()` is a shortcut for `geom_point(position = "jitter")`: it moves each point by a small
random amount, so that points which would sit on one another, as discrete values make them do, can
be told apart. Giggly has both, `Giggly.Plot.geom_jitter/3`, with `width:` and `height:`, and
`Giggly.Plot.position_jitter/1`, which `Giggly.Plot.geom_point/3`'s `position:` takes.

Giggly's jitter draws Erlang's random numbers, not R's, so its points land elsewhere than ggplot2's,
each within the same distance of its place, and an axis that widens to hold them can end a little
elsewhere too, by no more than about twice that distance; everything else in a drawing is ggplot2's.
Where ggplot2 draws new numbers each time a plot is drawn unless its position is given a `seed`,
Giggly draws the same points every time, from a seed of 0 unless `position_jitter/1`'s `seed:` gives
another.

A plot is described with `Giggly.Plot` and drawn with `Giggly.to_svg/2`, which returns an SVG
document. The setup cell aliases `Giggly.Plot`, so the cells write `Plot.new/2`, and defines
`render`, which draws a plot and shows it below the cell with `Kino.Image`. The data in these
examples is `Giggly.Datasets.mpg/0`, ggplot2's `mpg`: fuel economy for 234 cars of 38 popular models
from 1999 to 2008, which the setup cell binds to `mpg`, ggplot2's name for it.

## Points without jitter

```r
p <- ggplot(mpg, aes(cyl, hwy))
p + geom_point()
```

Many cars share both a number of cylinders and a highway mileage, so their points are drawn on top
of one another.

```elixir
p = Plot.new(mpg, %{x: :cyl, y: :hwy})

p
|> Plot.geom_point()
|> render.()
```

## Jittered points

```r
p + geom_jitter()
```

Each point moves up to 0.4 either way, 40% of the closest spacing of the values, which is 1 here,
as both columns hold whole numbers; both axes widen to hold the points.

```elixir
p
|> Plot.geom_jitter()
|> render.()
```

## Mapping colour

```r
# Add aesthetic mappings
p + geom_jitter(aes(colour = class))
```

```elixir
p
|> Plot.geom_jitter(%{colour: :class})
|> render.()
```

## Less jitter

```r
# Use smaller width/height to emphasise categories
ggplot(mpg, aes(cyl, hwy)) +
  geom_jitter()
ggplot(mpg, aes(cyl, hwy)) +
  geom_jitter(width = 0.25)
```

Unless told otherwise, the jitter moves a point up to 40% of the data's resolution either way;
a smaller `width` keeps each number of cylinders in a narrower column.

```elixir
mpg
|> Plot.new(%{x: :cyl, y: :hwy})
|> Plot.geom_jitter()
|> render.()
```

```elixir
mpg
|> Plot.new(%{x: :cyl, y: :hwy})
|> Plot.geom_jitter(width: 0.25)
|> render.()
```

## More jitter

```r
# Use larger width/height to completely smooth away discreteness
ggplot(mpg, aes(cty, hwy)) +
  geom_jitter()
ggplot(mpg, aes(cty, hwy)) +
  geom_jitter(width = 0.5, height = 0.5)
```

City and highway mileages are whole numbers, so half a mile per gallon either way fills the gaps
between them.

```elixir
mpg
|> Plot.new(%{x: :cty, y: :hwy})
|> Plot.geom_jitter()
|> render.()
```

```elixir
mpg
|> Plot.new(%{x: :cty, y: :hwy})
|> Plot.geom_jitter(width: 0.5, height: 0.5)
|> render.()
```